{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,30]],"date-time":"2026-06-30T13:56:49Z","timestamp":1782827809834,"version":"3.54.5"},"reference-count":77,"publisher":"American Association for the Advancement of Science (AAAS)","issue":"24","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["Sci. Adv."],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2022,6,17]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>\n            In recent years, the area affected by forest degradation in the Brazilian Amazon has frequently been higher than deforestation. From August 2006 to July 2019, the degraded area totaled 194,058 km\n            <jats:sup>2<\/jats:sup>\n            , representing almost two times the 99,630 km\n            <jats:sup>2<\/jats:sup>\n            deforested in the same period. The impacts of degradation include biodiversity loss and changes in the carbon stocks, affecting the CO\n            <jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>\n            balance and future climate changes. This paper aims to explore socioeconomic and environmental factors that influence forest degradation, project future scenarios, and assess the impact on the regional carbon balance, combining forest degradation and deforestation-related processes (clear-cut deforestation and secondary vegetation dynamics). We show that, while net CO\n            <jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>\n            emissions from 2020 to 2050 are 0.74 Gt CO\n            <jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>\n            in the Sustainable scenario, this value reached 22.63 Gt CO\n            <jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>\n            in the Fragmentation scenario, an increasingly plausible scenario given the recent trends in the region.\n          <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1126\/sciadv.abj3309","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2022,6,15]],"date-time":"2022-06-15T17:53:55Z","timestamp":1655315635000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":26,"title":["Projections of future forest degradation and CO\n            <sub>2<\/sub>\n            emissions for the Brazilian Amazon"],"prefix":"10.1126","volume":"8","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3731-5554","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Talita O.","family":"Assis","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"National Institute for Space Research (INPE), S\u00e3o Jos\u00e9 dos Campos, Brazil."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0683-1142","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Ana Paula D.","family":"Aguiar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National Institute for Space Research (INPE), S\u00e3o Jos\u00e9 dos Campos, Brazil."},{"name":"Stockholm Resilience Centre, Stockholm, Sweden."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1045-4316","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Celso","family":"von Randow","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National Institute for Space Research (INPE), S\u00e3o Jos\u00e9 dos Campos, Brazil."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5808-8784","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Carlos A.","family":"Nobre","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute for Advanced Studies, University of S\u00e3o Paulo, S\u00e3o Paulo, Brazil."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"221","reference":[{"key":"e_1_3_1_2_2","unstructured":"B. Turner D. Skole S. Sanderson G. Fischer L. Fresco R. Leemans \u201cLand-Use and Land-Cover Change (LUCC): Science\/Research Plan\u201d (HDP Report No. 7 1995)."},{"key":"e_1_3_1_3_2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1126\/science.1111772"},{"key":"e_1_3_1_4_2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/j.landusepol.2009.09.003"},{"key":"e_1_3_1_5_2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1080\/00167223.2011.10669524"},{"key":"e_1_3_1_6_2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1038\/19066"},{"key"